WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:09.520
On the 7th of January 1978 an article entitled&nbsp;
‘Iran and Red and Black Colonisation’ appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.640
in Ettela’at, an Iranian daily newspaper. It&nbsp;
put forward an implausible argument that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.080
conservative religious leaders of Iran were in&nbsp;
league with the Communists and were attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.160
to overthrow the government of the Shah, the&nbsp;
royal ruler of Iran. The article is widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.680
understood to have been written at the behest of&nbsp;
the Shah and ultimately sparked the first stages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:41.520
of the Iranian Revolution. Why was the Shah so&nbsp;
unpopular and what did he do to try to cling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:52.720
to power? This is the story of Shah Mohammed&nbsp;
Reza Pahlavi and the White Revolution.  
&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:12.320
The man known to history as Shah Mohammad Reza&nbsp;
Pahlavi was born on the 26th of October 1919&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.920
in the city of Tehran in Iran. Mohammad had&nbsp;
a twin-sister named Ashraf. His father was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:29.200
Reza Khan, though he is more widely known as&nbsp;
Reza Shah Pahlavi. Reza was born in 1878. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.320
joined the Persian military when he was 14&nbsp;
years old and became a rising officer in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:41.680
Persian Cossacks Brigade by his twenties. Reza&nbsp;
was interested in western culture and ideas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.120
was well connected by his mid-twenties with&nbsp;
the ambassadors and consuls representing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:55.680
European powers in Tehran. Reza married four&nbsp;
times. His second wife was Nimtaj Ayromlou,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:04.400
later known as Tadj ol-Molouk. They married in&nbsp;
1916. Mohammad had ten siblings and half-siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:14.240
but he was his father’s oldest son.
When Mohammad was born in 1919, the Qajar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:21.200
Dynasty was celebrating the 130th anniversary&nbsp;
of their rule over Iran. Persia, as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.200
western powers termed it, was one of the very few&nbsp;
countries outside of Europe and the Americas which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.440
managed to remain independent during the first age&nbsp;
of globalisation in the nineteenth century and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:40.160
accelerated wave of western imperialism that came&nbsp;
with it. This reflected Persia’s status as a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.800
centre of civilization stretching back to ancient&nbsp;
times. The Persian Empire between the mid-sixth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:55.280
and mid-fourth centuries BC had ruled from Egypt&nbsp;
and Turkey all the way east to Afghanistan. Later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.520
the Persians exerted substantial influence over&nbsp;
the Arab Caliphates that were ruled from Baghdad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.680
while Safavid Persia resisted the armies of&nbsp;
the Ottoman Empire in the sixteenth century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.280
In the nineteenth century, after the establishment&nbsp;
of the Qajar Dynasty, the country became a focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:18.720
of geopolitical rivalry between Russia and&nbsp;
Britain, with Russia controlling all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:26.720
the land north of Persia and east of the Caspian&nbsp;
Sea, and Britain ruling over India and Pakistan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.880
The ‘Great Game’, as this Russo-British rivalry&nbsp;
in Central Asia was known, came to an end in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:41.520
1890s and 1900s through several agreements between&nbsp;
Russia and Britain. Persia remained independent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:47.360
but Britain did have a substantial interest in&nbsp;
the country, particularly after the discovery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:55.200
large petroleum reserves here and the formation&nbsp;
in 1909 of the Anglo-Persian Oil Company.
&nbsp;

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:02.800
Although Persia had managed to remain&nbsp;
nominally independent under the Qajar Dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.720
and also tried to remain neutral in the&nbsp;
First World War when it broke out in 1914,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.320
the country’s geostrategic importance meant&nbsp;
that large parts of it were occupied between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:20.720
1914 and 1918 as the British and&nbsp;
Russians, who were now allies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.120
tried to defeat the Ottoman Empire in the Middle&nbsp;
East. Later the country was also impacted by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:35.040
spillover effect of the Russian Revolution and&nbsp;
Civil War from 1917 through to the early 1920s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:43.200
All of this instability led to a terrible&nbsp;
famine in Iran between 1917 and 1919. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.440
often overlooked and forgotten event led to the&nbsp;
deaths of an estimated two million Iranians.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:55.200
This, combined with the impact of the Spanish&nbsp;
Flu pandemic at the same time, and the way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.120
which the Qajar Dynasty had been damaged by the&nbsp;
occupation of the country during the war, led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:08.640
a military coup in Persia on the 22nd of February&nbsp;
1921. This was led by Mohammad’s father, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:15.760
clandestine British support. Reza Khan then became&nbsp;
the Minister of War and spent the next two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:23.440
quelling numerous local rebellions across Persia.&nbsp;
In 1923 he assumed the office of Prime Minister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:30.320
Finally, on the 15th of December 1925, the Qajar&nbsp;
Dynasty, which had been left in place after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:38.720
coup of 1921, were removed entirely and Reza Khan&nbsp;
ascended as the new Shah or king of Persia, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:46.640
the dynastic name Pahlavi. Ten years later, in&nbsp;
1935, he began issuing directives that henceforth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:53.280
the nation was to be known as Iran rather than&nbsp;
Persia, the latter of which is actually a Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:06:01.440
exonym for the entire Persian Empire rather&nbsp;
than the local name for the country. 
	
&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:07.120
Mohammad would barely have been aware of the&nbsp;
turmoil which surrounded him throughout his youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.440
He was born into a country suffering through civil&nbsp;
war, disease and famine, and grew up surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:19.600
military coups and political scheming that his&nbsp;
father was at the heart of. He had only recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.760
celebrated his sixth birthday when his father&nbsp;
became Shah, an event which saw Mohammad become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:32.560
the Crown Prince of Iran. His status meant that&nbsp;
his education was overseen closely by his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:39.120
who enlisted military officers as his tutors.&nbsp;
Although Reza had no major experience of Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:45.680
himself, he was aware of the need to modernise&nbsp;
Iran, and so he sent Mohammad off to attend a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:52.800
boarding school in Switzerland, the Institut le&nbsp;
Rosey. This was in 1931, when he was nearing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.840
twelfth birthday. Clearly, he arrived in Europe&nbsp;
with an elevated sense of himself as Crown Prince,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:05.520
but his years here were formative. Mohammad&nbsp;
developed a love of French culture and learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:12.160
speak several European languages. In later years&nbsp;
he and his third wife spoke with their children in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.960
French at home. His later modernisation programme&nbsp;
in Iran was partly due to his experiences during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:26.800
his teenage years in Europe. He returned home in&nbsp;
1936 to enter the Military Academy in Tehran. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.200
then it was clear that the world was becoming more&nbsp;
militaristic and violent again. Nazi Germany was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.560
rearming speedily after breaching the terms of&nbsp;
the Treaty of Versailles and would soon annex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:46.240
Austria and then Czechoslovakia in Europe. Italy&nbsp;
conquered Abyssinia via the Second Italo-Ethiopian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:55.200
War of 1935 to 1937, and Japan expanded&nbsp;
its plans to conquer China in 1937, having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:01.840
previously annexed Korea and created the puppet&nbsp;
state of Manchukuo in Manchuria. The outbreak of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:08.800
the Second World War in September 1939 after&nbsp;
Germany invaded Poland would lead to Mohammad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:18.320
becoming Shah of Iran sooner than expected. 
Mohammad’s father adopted a policy of neutrality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:24.720
following the outbreak of the war. However, over&nbsp;
time, Iran was dragged into the conflict more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:30.480
more. It was strategically important because&nbsp;
of its oil supplies, which Nazi Germany was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.560
anxious to acquire, and it also became central&nbsp;
to Britain’s campaign to supply the Soviet Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:43.600
after the Germans invaded the USSR in the summer&nbsp;
of 1941. Within days of the commencement of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:49.840
war between the Soviets and the Nazis, there were&nbsp;
discussions in London, Moscow and Berlin about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:56.240
strategic necessity of securing control over Iran&nbsp;
for both sides. The Germans were hoping to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.080
Mohammad’s father into the Axis alliance, but the&nbsp;
Soviets and British acted decisively before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:08.000
could do so. They felt they needed to act as&nbsp;
Reza had adopted an increasingly independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:14.320
position in the 1930s and was anxious to break&nbsp;
away from British and Russian influence in Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:22.240
Asia and the Middle East. Fearing that he might&nbsp;
ally with Germany, on the 25th of August 1941 a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:29.920
join Anglo-Soviet campaign was launched to invade&nbsp;
Iran and take over the country’s key apparatus. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.760
ceasefire was agreed to, five days later, and the&nbsp;
Iranian army collapsed in the face of the Allied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:42.480
invasion. Reza Khan was sent a polite message by&nbsp;
the British two weeks later instructing him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:49.280
kindly abdicate in favour of his son. The British&nbsp;
had briefly considered whether it would be best to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:56.320
reinstall the Qajar Dynasty that Reza had removed&nbsp;
from power back in 1925, but in the end, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:03.440
decided that maintaining the Pahlavi Dynasty was&nbsp;
as satisfactory, if Mohammad proved to be biddable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:09.840
for so long as the war was underway. Reza had no&nbsp;
option except to do as instructed, which meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:17.200
that on the 16th of September 1941, six weeks&nbsp;
before his 22nd birthday, Mohammad became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:27.200
Shah of Iran. His father went into exile and died&nbsp;
in Johannesburg in South Africa in July 1944.
&nbsp;

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:36.320
The new, young Shah in Tehran had no independence&nbsp;
of action in the first years of his reign. Iran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:42.480
had effectively been occupied by the Allied Powers&nbsp;
and remained under Allied occupation until the war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:48.960
ended. Iran was even host to the first major&nbsp;
meeting of the ‘Big Three’ Allied leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.480
the British Prime Minister, Winston Churchill,&nbsp;
the Soviet dictator, Joseph Stalin, and the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:11:01.680
President, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, between the&nbsp;
28th of November and the 1st of December 1943.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:06.480
While the Shah was involved in a ceremonial&nbsp;
capacity in the governance of Iran between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:13.840
September 1941 and the end of the war in 1945,&nbsp;
the real rulers of the country were the British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:21.120
and Soviet ambassadors and their staff. Ahmad&nbsp;
Qavam, an Iranian politician who had served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:27.920
as Prime Minister back in the early 1920s after&nbsp;
the coup of 1921, returned to the office of Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:33.920
Minister during the war and was actually more&nbsp;
influential than the young Shah. Mohammad grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:40.160
to resent Qavam’s power. The shift in the power&nbsp;
dynamic between them only started once the war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:48.000
ended in Europe in May 1945. A crisis erupted in&nbsp;
the Middle East after Stalin was slow to withdraw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.160
the Red Army from Iranian territory. A prolonged&nbsp;
dispute over the border region between Iran and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:00.800
Azerbaijan provided the opportunity for the Shah&nbsp;
to push Qavam out of the office of Prime Minister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:07.680
and to assert his own position as ruler of&nbsp;
the country. Thus, from late 1947 onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:14.480
the Shah began to transition from a puppet ruler&nbsp;
of a militarily-occupied country to an independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:23.120
ruler of a post-war sovereign nation.
This capacity for asserting his independence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:29.200
was reflected in the Shah’s personal life.&nbsp;
Shortly after he returned to Iran from Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:35.920
back in 1936, his father had entered negotiations&nbsp;
with the Egyptian royal family to organise a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:41.520
dynastic alliance between Egypt and Iran&nbsp;
by marrying Mohammad to Princess Fawzia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:49.680
the daughter of King Fuad I of Egypt and sister of&nbsp;
King Farouk I who ascended to the throne in 1936.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.840
From a dynastic perspective this was an excellent&nbsp;
alliance for the upstart Pahlavi Dynasty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.840 --> 00:13:00.880
which was looking to cement its place among&nbsp;
the royal houses of the Middle East, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:07.680
was not a happy marriage. They had a daughter&nbsp;
together in 1940, Princess Shahnaz, following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:14.800
which they became increasingly estranged. Once&nbsp;
the war began to wind down in Allied victory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:21.520
Fawzia returned to Egypt, where she obtained a&nbsp;
divorce under Egyptian law. The Shah obtained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:31.280
a separate Iranian divorce in 1948. He then&nbsp;
married Soraya Esfandiary-Bakhtiary in 1951.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:39.040
This second marriage ended in divorce too in 1958&nbsp;
after the union failed to result in any children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:46.080
The Shah needed a legitimate male heir to continue&nbsp;
the dynastic line and so he divorced Soraya and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:55.040
married Farah Diba in 1959. This third marriage&nbsp;
produced four children, two boys, Reza and Ali,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:02.160
and two daughters, Farahnaz and Leila. The claim&nbsp;
that the Shah was possibly gay, based on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:08.720
very close friendship with the Swiss man, Ernest&nbsp;
Perron, has been put forward in several studies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:13.840
They met while the Shah was studying in&nbsp;
Switzerland in his teenage years and Perron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:20.800
became a powerful courtier and official in Iran&nbsp;
after the war. While Perron was clearly gay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:26.880
and the position he obtained in Iran based on&nbsp;
his close relationship with the Shah was unusual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:33.040
there is no concrete evidence to imply&nbsp;
the Shah was gay or bisexual. 
&nbsp;

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:41.280
Perron fell from power at the Shah’s court owing&nbsp;
to his role in the Abadan Crisis. This was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:49.680
name given to the general period of crisis in&nbsp;
Iran’s politics between 1951 and 1954. Abadan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:55.200
was the centre of the oil-extraction industry&nbsp;
in the country. The crisis was sparked by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:02.480
assassination on the 7th of March 1951 of the&nbsp;
Iranian Prime Minister, Haj Ali Razmara, by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:08.640
Islamic fundamentalist. Although this foreshadowed&nbsp;
later clashes between conservative religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:14.160
elements in Iran and the Shah’s government,&nbsp;
the immediate cause of the assassination was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:20.320
a widespread belief that Razmara was about to&nbsp;
sign an unfavourable deal with the British that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:25.360
would extend the rights of the Anglo-Iranian&nbsp;
Oil Company in the country, which was perceived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:33.280
as a legacy of the European age of imperialism.&nbsp;
Razmara was succeeded by Mohammad Mossadegh. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:39.920
Prime Minister he was the driving force behind the&nbsp;
nationalisation of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:46.400
This in turn created two years of tensions as the&nbsp;
British argued to their American allies that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:51.840
nationalisation of the oil industry was a sign&nbsp;
that Iran was about to fall into the Soviet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:58.800
communist camp in the intensifying Cold War.&nbsp;
Although it is very doubtful that this was likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:05.680
British resentments ultimately resulted in a&nbsp;
western-backed coup in August 1953. While it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:12.720
occurring the Shah fled Iran, flying to Europe. He&nbsp;
remained in Rome until the unrest had died down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.680
spending much of his time in the nightclubs&nbsp;
of the Italian capital and waiting to see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:23.440
would happen back home. Even supporters of the&nbsp;
Shah condemned his flight to Europe as an act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:29.360
of cowardice, but when he returned to Iran his&nbsp;
position was stronger than it had ever been,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:35.600
as the western powers were content to have a&nbsp;
strong Shah in Iran who would act as a bulwark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:41.360
against the growing pan-Arabism elsewhere in&nbsp;
the Middle East, which had a socialist ideology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:48.240
and leaned more towards Moscow on account of&nbsp;
Washington’s support for the state of Israel.
&nbsp;

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:56.320
The Shah became a much more authoritarian ruler&nbsp;
following his restoration. It must be admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.320 --> 00:17:02.240
that he was overseeing a country that was very&nbsp;
difficult to rule. Iran is often viewed as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:07.600
homogenous state, but it is actually a patchwork&nbsp;
of different ethnic and religious groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:12.960
with the Balochs in the southeast and the&nbsp;
Azeris and Kurds in the northwest all being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:19.520
inclined towards regional separatism. On top of&nbsp;
this, the more secular policies which the Shah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:24.720
and his father before him, favoured had brought&nbsp;
the Pahlavi Dynasty into conflict with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:30.960
conservative religious leaders of the country&nbsp;
as early as the 1930s. Add to this the tensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:37.920
created by the crisis of the early 1950s and the&nbsp;
experience of having to flee the country in 1953,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:44.400
and the Shah was consequently inclined to adopt&nbsp;
a more authoritarian approach by the mid-1950s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:50.320
Press censorship and the monitoring of religious&nbsp;
groups and leaders became commonplace. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:57.760
in 1957, the Bureau for Intelligence and Security&nbsp;
of the State, better known by its acronym, SAVAK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:05.440
was set up. This secret police force expanded&nbsp;
through the late 1950s and 1960s to become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:12.240
small, secretive army with thousands of members.&nbsp;
It has been observed by many commentators that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:18.240
Shah’s SAVAK paved the way for later militias&nbsp;
and secret police forces established by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:24.800
Islamic Republic of Iran. While there is no&nbsp;
denying that SAVAK was a brutal regime that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:30.960
killed thousands of people and detained&nbsp;
and tortured many others over 22 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:36.640
its activities pale in comparison to the murder&nbsp;
and illegal detention of hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:43.680
of people by modern Iranian security services&nbsp;
such as the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:53.200
Nevertheless, SAVAK’s conduct remains a black mark&nbsp;
on the record of the Shah as ruler of Iran.
&nbsp;

00:18:53.200 --> 00:19:00.080
In 1963 the Shah began a programme of&nbsp;
modernisation known as the White Revolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:06.320
This was not entirely novel. All the way back&nbsp;
in the 1930s, his father had begun introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:13.600
measures to orientate Iran towards becoming a more&nbsp;
western-style country. For instance, industrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:19.200
advisors were brought in to begin setting up&nbsp;
factories and providing technical knowledge for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:25.440
new Iranian businesses and companies. There was&nbsp;
also a drive to break down the traditional pillars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:31.840
of Iranian society, religion and the social&nbsp;
hierarchy that went back hundreds of years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:38.880
As a Francophile who was educated in Europe,&nbsp;
the Shah wanted to go even further. In 1963,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:45.280
a referendum was put to the Iranian people to&nbsp;
commence a sweeping range of reforms. It passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:52.640
in a landslide, primarily because it was boycotted&nbsp;
by those who opposed the proposed reforms. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:58.800
over the next fifteen years, under the auspices&nbsp;
of the White Revolution, huge amounts of land that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.800 --> 00:20:04.800
belonged to the traditional aristocracy, tribal&nbsp;
chiefs and the powerful Shia Muslim clerics was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:11.040
taken into state ownership and then redistributed&nbsp;
to farmers and the lower-middle class. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:17.680
also a very considerable investment of Iran’s oil&nbsp;
revenue into expanding the education system and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:25.280
improving the country’s infrastructure. Literacy&nbsp;
levels improved markedly. In the 1950s only 15% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:32.960
Iranians were literate but by the end of the 1970s&nbsp;
nearly 50% of the population could read and write.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:38.720
There were political reforms too. Women were given&nbsp;
the right to vote in Iranian elections at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:45.600
start of the White Revolution. Healthcare access&nbsp;
was expanded. A Health Corps was set up which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:55.440
trained around 4,500 groups of medical officials&nbsp;
to offer local healthcare around Iran.
&nbsp;

00:20:55.440 --> 00:21:01.680
As positive as many of the outcomes from the&nbsp;
White Revolution were, the reforms were opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:08.560
by a conservative element in Iranian society.&nbsp;
Iran is a complex country. On the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:15.360
its sophisticated culture and history going back&nbsp;
to ancient times has made it a learned society and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:21.760
one of the most liberal in the entire Middle East.&nbsp;
On the other hand, there is a strong conservative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:28.800
ultra-religious element. That duality exists in&nbsp;
Iran in the early twenty-first century and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:35.040
there too in the Shah’s time. In the 1960s,&nbsp;
the closest thing which the conservative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:42.560
religious faction had to a leader was Ayatollah&nbsp;
Ruhollah Khomeini, a senior Twelver Shia cleric.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:50.560
The opposition which the Shah’s reforms of 1963&nbsp;
created turned Khomeini into a national figure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.560 --> 00:21:57.200
In January 1963, when the White Revolution&nbsp;
started, Khomeini began giving speeches around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:04.080
Iran in which he denounced the Shah and condemned&nbsp;
the reforms which were being initiated. He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:10.400
led the boycott of the referendum which the&nbsp;
Shah called on the reform programme. Then, on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:17.840
the 3rd of June, Khomeini compared the Shah to the&nbsp;
Caliph Yazid, an early ruler of the Arab Caliphate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:23.360
back in the seventh century, who played a role in&nbsp;
the split within the Muslim world into Sunni and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:30.320
Shiite camps. Khomeini was basically saying that&nbsp;
the Shah was a divisive figure who was splitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:36.880
Iran into two camps through his aggressive&nbsp;
modernisation. The comparison also questioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:43.840
the Shah’s legitimacy as ruler of Iran. The Shah&nbsp;
ordered Khomeini to be arrested two days later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:51.200
a move which led to major protests and riots in&nbsp;
which around 400 people were killed. Khomeini was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:56.720
placed under house arrest. When he was released,&nbsp;
he returned to giving provocative speeches against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:03.920
the Shah and in the end he was arrested again and&nbsp;
banished in November 1964 when he was placed on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:10.960
flight to Turkey. He quickly relocated to Iraq and&nbsp;
spent the next thirteen years based in the city of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:18.800
Najaf there, becoming a focus of opposition to&nbsp;
the Shah just over Iran’s western border. 
&nbsp;

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:25.680
The growing schism between conservative&nbsp;
religious forces in Iran and a more secular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:30.720
liberal community that looked towards the&nbsp;
west was exacerbated by the economic growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:36.720
of Iran. It is a basic fact of modern politics&nbsp;
that leaders can bring a certain part of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:42.960
population along with them if there is economic&nbsp;
growth in their country. The reforms overseen by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:48.240
the Shah’s government ushered in the greatest&nbsp;
period of economic growth in Iranian history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:57.040
GDP growth in the 1960s and 1970s averaged&nbsp;
nearly 10% per year. Without a doubt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.040 --> 00:24:04.240
this was based to a considerable degree on oil&nbsp;
revenue, especially after the 1973 Oil Crisis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:09.440
when the average cost of a barrel of oil on the&nbsp;
international markets quadrupled in the space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.440 --> 00:24:16.800
of six months from October 1973 onwards. But oil&nbsp;
revenue does not always lead to national economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:16.800 --> 00:24:23.120
growth if the profits are mismanaged. The Shah’s&nbsp;
government oversaw an intelligent re-investment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:29.200
oil revenue to develop other parts of the economy&nbsp;
such as the production of textiles, cement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:36.880
petrochemicals, fertilisers, and cash crops&nbsp;
like sugar, dates and pistachios. In this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:42.640
Iran set an early template for economic&nbsp;
diversification from oil revenue that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:50.400
subsequently adopted by the Gulf States and Saudi&nbsp;
Arabia. Living standards jumped. A better-educated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:57.040
and more affluent cohort emerged in Iran’s cities&nbsp;
who dressed like they lived in Paris or Rome, went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.040 --> 00:25:03.040
to the cinema to watch western-produced films and&nbsp;
acted very differently to how their contemporaries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:03.040 --> 00:25:10.640
did in places like Saudi Arabia in the 1960s and&nbsp;
1970s. Underneath all of this economic change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:19.920
though, was a society that was divided over the&nbsp;
White Revolution for a multitude of reasons.
&nbsp;

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:25.920
In the short term, this new prosperity led to&nbsp;
a growing centralisation of power at the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:32.320
court, where the Shah had an increasingly&nbsp;
elevated sense of his own self-importance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:38.400
Beginning in the mid-1960s he started to grant&nbsp;
himself a series of evermore elaborate titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:44.720
which is rarely a good sign of things to come in&nbsp;
an autocratic state. For instance, on the 15th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:51.680
September 1965 the announcement came that the&nbsp;
Shah would be known as Aryamehr, a word which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:58.000
means ‘Light of the Aryans’, a reference to the&nbsp;
pseudo-scientific view that the vast array of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:04.880
people who speak Indo-European languages, from&nbsp;
England all the way east to Iran and India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:12.400
are descended from a root ‘Aryan’ people. The&nbsp;
Shah also held the title of Bozorg Arteshtaran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:20.560
meaning ‘Great Army Leader’. In 1967, not content&nbsp;
with being a king, he proclaimed that he was now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:27.760
Shahanshah, which means ‘King of Kings’. The&nbsp;
elevation of his title was accompanied by a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:27.760 --> 00:26:35.760
formal coronation on the 26th of October that&nbsp;
year. Finally, in mid-October 1971, the Shah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:43.040
presided over the 2500-year celebrations of the&nbsp;
founding of the Persian Empire in ancient times by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:50.720
Cyrus the Great. These festivities were held over&nbsp;
a week in a tent city erected at Persepolis, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:57.440
was attended by heads of state from abroad and&nbsp;
other royal families. The dating was questionable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.440 --> 00:27:05.760
Cyrus the Great had conquered the Babylonian&nbsp;
Empire in 539 BC, so it would have made more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:14.720
sense to hold this 2500-year anniversary in 1961.&nbsp;
Ultimately it, along with his expanding array of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:21.840
titles, was a sign of the Shah’s accelerating&nbsp;
sense of his own importance and power.
&nbsp;

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:31.600
There was also an evolving foreign policy adopted&nbsp;
by the Shah in the 1960s and 1970s. The Pahlavi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.600 --> 00:27:37.040
Dynasty’s foreign relations had shifted&nbsp;
repeatedly. The desire to break away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:37.040 --> 00:27:42.400
British dominance of the oil industry in the&nbsp;
Middle East had led to some scepticism about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:50.640
western influences in Iran, which peaked in the&nbsp;
Abadan Crisis and the coup of 1953. Despite later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:57.040
interpretations of the Shah as being a classic&nbsp;
Cold War-era strongman aligned with Washington,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:04.000
the relationship was more complex. The US&nbsp;
and British intervention in 1953 led to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:10.480
new arrangement whereby Iran’s oil industry&nbsp;
was placed into a trust controlled by various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:16.800
international entities. Because of this, the Shah&nbsp;
and his government were tied to the western powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:24.480
for the next 19 years but he demonstrated his&nbsp;
independence of action in March 1973 when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:30.800
nationalised all foreign oil interests, nullifying&nbsp;
the actions of the British and Americans twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:37.360
years earlier. Although Saudi Arabia led the&nbsp;
oil embargo on western nations later that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:37.360 --> 00:28:43.680
Iran benefited greatly from the pursuant price&nbsp;
increase. In terms of his diplomatic stances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:49.760
throughout the Middle East, the Shah adopted a&nbsp;
much more conciliatory line towards Israel than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:56.640
the vast majority of other Muslim nations. This&nbsp;
was in return for US support in Iran’s regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.640 --> 00:29:03.920
rivalry with Iraq. More broadly, as his reign&nbsp;
went on, he cultivated strong ties to the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:09.680
Britain and France, particularly with leaders&nbsp;
like Charles de Gaulle in France and the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:16.720
President Richard Nixon. 
	
Part of the Shah’s foreign policy outlook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:22.880
was shaped by a desire for energy diversification&nbsp;
and military power in the Middle East and Central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.880 --> 00:29:31.280
Asia. The 1950s, 1960s and 1970s were an era&nbsp;
in which nuclear power was a favoured source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:31.280 --> 00:29:37.840
of energy production. The Shah’s government began&nbsp;
developing a nuclear programme in the late 1950s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:45.360
with technical advice from the Americans, French&nbsp;
and West Germans. In 1967 the Tehran Research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:51.760
Reactor became operational. Like so many other&nbsp;
nations of the era, as soon as enriched uranium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:56.960
was present in the country for the purposes of&nbsp;
producing domestic energy, thoughts turned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.960 --> 00:30:04.400
how to produce a nuclear weapon. By 1974 the US&nbsp;
State Department was concerned that the Iranians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:09.760
were looking to use enriched plutonium to&nbsp;
manufacture one. They were also anxious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:14.880
about the proliferation of nuclear material&nbsp;
around the country and concerns were expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:21.760
in Washington about what might happen to these&nbsp;
resources if Iran experienced a new revolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:29.520
invasion or change of government.
It was understandable that the administrations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:35.680
of both President Gerald Ford and President&nbsp;
Jimmy Carter were concerned about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:42.960
The Shah started behaving from 1974 onwards&nbsp;
in ways that led to rising unrest in Iran.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:48.880
This is surprising on some level. Rapidly rising&nbsp;
oil revenue and the nationalisation of foreign&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.880 --> 00:30:55.520
interests in the oil industry in 1973 meant that&nbsp;
the coffers of the Shah’s government were never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.520 --> 00:31:04.480
fuller but the unprecedented amount of money&nbsp;
at his disposal led to hubris. In March 1975,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:10.800
Mohammad turned Iran into a one-party state&nbsp;
when he set up the new Rastakhiz Party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:16.720
or ‘Resurrection’ Party, and made it the&nbsp;
sole legal political party in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:24.160
merging multiple existing political parties into&nbsp;
it. More worryingly, rampant corruption developed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:28.880
Billions of dollars’ worth of oil revenue&nbsp;
were siphoned off into the hands of the Shah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.880 --> 00:31:35.520
his family and a close coterie of aristocrats&nbsp;
and politicians. This uneven distribution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:42.080
Iran’s newfound wealth was compounded by steeply&nbsp;
rising inflation as money poured into the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:48.880
without proper fiscal management. Inflation rose&nbsp;
to an average of about 15% per year in the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.880 --> 00:31:56.480
half of the 1970s. Corruption seeped into everyday&nbsp;
life, with government officials, police and court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:56.480 --> 00:32:03.440
officers all requiring bribes to serve the public&nbsp;
interest. Opposition to the regime spiralled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:09.760
amongst both left-wing, secular liberals and much&nbsp;
more conservative religious leaders who looked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.760 --> 00:32:16.960
Ayatollah Khomeini in exile across the border in&nbsp;
Iraq as a leader of their movement. 
&nbsp;

00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:23.520
The Iranian Revolution began with the&nbsp;
publication of the article ‘Iran and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:30.720
Red and Black Colonisation’ in Ettela’at&nbsp;
in January 1978, most likely at the behest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:30.720 --> 00:32:38.000
of the Shah. It escalated incrementally over&nbsp;
the next year. The deaths of an estimated 450&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:44.800
people in the Cinema Rex Fire on the 19th&nbsp;
of August 1978 was a major turning point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:51.280
The Shah did try to respond as opposition&nbsp;
demonstrations intensified around the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:56.880
drawing hundreds of thousands of people out&nbsp;
onto the streets. For instance, in October&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.880 --> 00:33:03.520
1978 the Shah announced the dissolution of the&nbsp;
short-lived Rastakhiz Party, hoping that political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:10.320
liberalisation would quell the unrest. He also&nbsp;
pressured the Iraqi government to expel Khomeini.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:10.320 --> 00:33:16.960
The Ayatollah briefly resettled in France and&nbsp;
waited for the opportune moment to return to Iran.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:23.680
These measures were ineffective and unprecedented&nbsp;
rallies in December 1978 led to upwards of ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:29.440
million people taking to the streets to show their&nbsp;
dislike of the regime and signal the need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:29.440 --> 00:33:36.640
change. By then the Shah was becoming increasingly&nbsp;
desperate and paranoid. He attacked British and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:42.320
US diplomats in Tehran and claimed they were&nbsp;
clandestinely supporting the protestors in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:48.640
the hope of displacing him. Once street fighting&nbsp;
commenced between the government and protestors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:55.680
the regime was on borrowed time. Word soon reached&nbsp;
Mohammad that leading generals were in discussions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.680 --> 00:34:04.240
with the opposition. On the 16th of January 1979,&nbsp;
the Shah flew out of Iran, never to return. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:11.520
weeks later, on the 1st of February, the Ayatollah&nbsp;
arrived home after fifteen years in exile. Intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:16.960
debates over the future trajectory of the country&nbsp;
followed and it was only in December that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:25.040
Ayatollah was confirmed as the first Supreme&nbsp;
Leader of the new Islamic Republic of Iran.
&nbsp;

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:32.160
The narrative will always be that the Shah&nbsp;
fled from Iran as the opposition to his rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:38.640
reached a crescendo and he was in danger of being&nbsp;
overthrown and detained if he didn’t leave the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:38.640 --> 00:34:45.760
country in January 1979. In this light, the cover&nbsp;
story that the Shah was ill and was travelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:52.720
abroad for health reasons is seen as a smokescreen&nbsp;
however two things can be right at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:59.840
The Shah was genuinely very ill. He had been&nbsp;
suffering from chronic lymphocytic leukaemia since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:08.000
around 1974, a type of blood cancer. By the time&nbsp;
he went into exile for the second time in 1979,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:14.320
his condition had sharply deteriorated. Indeed,&nbsp;
western leaders perhaps did not back him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:20.400
strongly as they otherwise might have because they&nbsp;
believed he would not live much longer. What he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:26.400
did after leaving Iran was therefore unwise.&nbsp;
Instead of immediately seeking out the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:32.320
healthcare he could, which the family certainly&nbsp;
had the finances to do, he spent months travelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:39.920
across North Africa and then to Central America.&nbsp;
Mohammad spent time in Egypt, Morocco, Panama and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:39.920 --> 00:35:47.680
Mexico. When he was finally examined in Mexico by&nbsp;
a high-level physician, Dr. Benjamin Kean, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:55.520
were drastic. He had developed severe gallstones&nbsp;
and had multiple tumours. At that stage, President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.520 --> 00:36:02.080
Jimmy Carter was convinced to allow the Shah to&nbsp;
enter the US on compassionate grounds. Carter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:06.800
had previously been reluctant to do anything that&nbsp;
might look like he was granting the Shah political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:14.160
asylum in the US. He was receiving treatment in&nbsp;
America by October 1979, but surgery revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:19.920
the extent to which his cancer had progressed&nbsp;
and showed that his condition was now terminal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:28.080
In early 1980 his health continued to decline as&nbsp;
infections set in. Undeterred, the Shah continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:36.240
to travel, returning to Egypt. He ultimately&nbsp;
died in Cairo on the 27th of July 1980 at 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.240 --> 00:36:42.480
years of age. With the family unable to bury&nbsp;
him back in Iran, his remains were laid to rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:52.720
in the Al Rifa’i Mosque in Cairo in Egypt. 
Iran has gone through peaks and mostly valleys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.720 --> 00:36:58.480
since the Shah’s time as ruler of the country.&nbsp;
The relationship between Iran and the western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:04.960
powers collapsed after the Revolution owing&nbsp;
to the kidnapping of 66 Americans by Islamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:10.880
extremists with connections to the government at&nbsp;
the US embassy in Tehran on the 4th of November&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.880 --> 00:37:18.960
1979. Over fifty of these were held hostage for&nbsp;
nearly 15 months. By the time the hostage crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:27.120
ended in January 1981, the relationship between&nbsp;
Iran and the US was hostile. It never recovered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:35.280
The US supported Saddam Hussein and the Baathists&nbsp;
in the Iran-Iraq War that ran from 1980 to 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:41.840
although there was a peculiar underhand attempt&nbsp;
to supply Iran with weapons via the Iran-Contra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:49.360
affair in the mid-1980s. The Ayatollah died&nbsp;
in 1989 and was succeeded by Ayatollah Ali&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:57.120
Khamenei as the Second Supreme Leader of Iran.&nbsp;
Under his rule there was a window in which Iran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:57.120 --> 00:38:06.400
could have changed course. Between 1997 and 2005&nbsp;
the Prime Minister, Mohammad Khatami, led a reform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.400 --> 00:38:13.280
movement. The turning point came in the mid-2000s&nbsp;
as the Ayatollah and the mullahs clamped down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:20.320
the reform movement. Ever since then there have&nbsp;
been protests against the regime every few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.320 --> 00:38:30.080
notably in 2009, 2017, 2023 and 2026. To cement&nbsp;
the position of the religious authorities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.080 --> 00:38:36.320
the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps and other&nbsp;
security forces were expanded to include hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:42.400
of thousands of personnel. Meanwhile, on the&nbsp;
international front, Iran remained under heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:48.000
sanctions which reduced the amount of income&nbsp;
it could receive from the sale of its oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:54.560
while Iran’s support for Hezbollah in Lebanon and&nbsp;
the Houthis in Yemen, along with other regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:54.560 --> 00:39:01.200
proxies, made it a constant enemy of Israel.&nbsp;
The US stance towards the country fluctuated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:09.680
though there was unanimity that Iran could not&nbsp;
be allowed to acquire a nuclear weapon.
&nbsp;

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:16.640
As this torturous relationship between Iran&nbsp;
and the west played out in the 1980s and 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:23.360
the Shah’s eldest son and claimant to the dormant&nbsp;
title of Shah, Reza Pahlavi, decided to move to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:23.360 --> 00:39:29.760
the United States after first living in Egypt&nbsp;
and Morocco for several years after his father’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:36.880
death. He had become a fulcrum for conspiracies&nbsp;
concerning the Iranian regime. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:42.880
in the 1980s he was in contact with the Israeli&nbsp;
government and elements in Saudi Arabia, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:50.000
is opposed to Shiite Iran on religious grounds&nbsp;
and because it views it as a regional rival.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:56.720
By the end of the 1980s, Pahlavi was presenting&nbsp;
himself as the voice of the Iranian opposition in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:02.160
exile and a leader of the Iranian American&nbsp;
community, which grew considerably in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:09.200
1980s as a result of the exodus following the&nbsp;
Revolution. As protests within Iran generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:09.200 --> 00:40:16.640
national attention from 2009 onwards, Pahlavi&nbsp;
became more prominent, especially during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:24.880
US-Israeli attacks on Iran in June 2025 and&nbsp;
the spring of 2026. For instance, following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:31.920
the killing of Ayatollah Khamenei on the 28th of&nbsp;
February 2026 there was speculation that Pahlavi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.920 --> 00:40:38.880
could become involved in forming a new government&nbsp;
in Iran. However, opinion polls and studies over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:45.440
the years have consistently demonstrated that&nbsp;
while Pahlavi might be well liked amongst the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:45.440 --> 00:40:51.920
exile community, the Shah’s son lacks the kind&nbsp;
of internal support base within Iran that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:57.520
translate into a restoration of the monarchy&nbsp;
some day in the future, though he clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:57.520 --> 00:41:07.680
aspires to a leadership role of some kind. 
The Shah of Iran’s legacy is complicated. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:12.880
was a man who came to power under difficult&nbsp;
circumstances, who often tested his relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:19.360
with the western powers, yet managed to retain&nbsp;
their support, and who is today viewed, somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.360 --> 00:41:26.640
paradoxically, as both a modernising reformer and&nbsp;
also an authoritarian. All of these things can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:32.880
true at the same time. The Shah’s rise to power&nbsp;
in succession to his father occurred because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:39.600
the unusual position Iran was in during the Second&nbsp;
World War and the desire of both the Germans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:47.040
the Allies to acquire control over the country and&nbsp;
its vital oil supply. In the aftermath of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:53.120
the Shah was able to retain the support of the&nbsp;
United States in the context of the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:59.120
even after Iran nationalised the Anglo-Iranian&nbsp;
Oil Company, an inflammatory action so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:05.200
as Britain was concerned. The simple reality&nbsp;
was that his actions were tolerated when set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:05.200 --> 00:42:11.440
against the backdrop of the Cold War, the rise&nbsp;
of socialist pan-Arab states in the Middle East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:17.440
and the fact that his regime was one of the few&nbsp;
ones in the entire region that was not bitterly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:24.640
opposed to Israel. He also did initiate reforms&nbsp;
that improved life in Iran for a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:30.160
such as increasing literacy levels and he was&nbsp;
also responsible for a period of huge economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.160 --> 00:42:36.720
growth. The country also became the most liberal&nbsp;
in the Middle East. People in countries like Saudi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:36.720 --> 00:42:43.360
Arabia, Qatar and the United Arab Emirates did not&nbsp;
enjoy anything near the liberties that Iranians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:50.560
did in the 1970s. Moreover, with oil prices&nbsp;
turbocharging Iran’s economic growth from late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:58.080
1973 onwards, the Shah should have been popular&nbsp;
and successful. His failing was in developing an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:04.720
overly authoritarian method of ruling and engaging&nbsp;
in rampant corruption and financial mismanagement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.720 --> 00:43:11.920
along with completely misjudging the political&nbsp;
atmosphere in the 1970s. Then again, maybe this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.920 --> 00:43:18.880
challenge was beyond the ability of any leader.&nbsp;
The same forces of liberalism and conservatism, of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:25.840
secularism and religious zealotry, which the Shah&nbsp;
failed to contain in the 1970s, have characterised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:35.040
Iran’s turbulent politics ever since. 
What do you think of the Shah? Was he a liberal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:41.360
ruler who was building a secular, modern Iran&nbsp;
before the Revolution led to his overthrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:41.360 --> 00:43:48.160
or was he a corrupt authoritarian who caused the&nbsp;
Revolution through his abuses of power? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

